# agnostic/engine/audit — the tamper-evident trail, on libro over patra.
#
# ## What this gives you, stated exactly
#
# Every entry carries the hash of its predecessor, so **modifying or reordering
# a persisted entry is detectable** by `agnostic_audit_verify`. That is real
# tamper-evidence and it is what M4 delivers.
#
# ⚠ **It is not signature-backed, and the reason is structural.** libro's
# `audit_entries` table is `(id, ts, sev, src, act, det, aid, phash, hash, halg)`
# — there is **no signature column**. `sign_entry` exists and produces a detached
# signature, but `patrastore_append` has nowhere to put it, so signing here would
# compute a signature and throw it away. Computing security theatre is worse than
# not computing it, so this module does not sign.
#
# The consequence, plainly: an attacker who can write the audit file **and**
# recompute every hash forward from the point they edit is not detected. Closing
# that needs either a signature column in libro or external anchoring (libro has
# `anchor_*`), and neither is M4.
#
# This is still strictly better than what the engine's own chain offers.
# `agnosai_audit_chain_new` mints its key with `random_bytes` on every start and
# keeps entries in a `vec` — so the engine's trail is process-lifetime and its
# signatures are unverifiable across a restart. Agnostic's is durable.
#
# ## ⚠ A streaming chain, because a retaining one grows forever
#
# `chain_new_streaming` keeps only the head hash. libro's own comment on it is
# the reason: for a write-through consumer — append, persist, never read the
# chain again — the retaining chain's entry vec "is pure accumulation, and there
# was no way to bound it ... A long-lived writer grew forever." A server is
# exactly that consumer. Linkage is byte-identical either way, so the durable
# chain verifies the same.
#
# The trade libro names is real and accepted: on a streaming chain `chain_len` is
# 0 and every in-memory query sees nothing. Reads go to the store.
#
# ⚠ A new streaming chain's head is empty, so the open seeds it from the last
# stored entry (`_agnostic_audit_seed_head_locked`). Without that, each process's
# first entry claimed to be the genesis and the next start reported tampering.
#
# ## ⚠ Gaps are invisible to verification, so they are counted instead
#
# Verification proves the entries that ARE there form an unbroken chain. It
# cannot show an entry that was never written — a failed append leaves no hole to
# find. So an append failure is logged at ERROR **and counted**, and the count is
# part of what an operator reads. A chain that verifies clean with a non-zero
# drop count has not told you everything.
#
# ⚠ **A failed write puts the head back (0.1.15).** `chain_append` moves the head to
# the new entry before `patrastore_append` runs. Until 0.1.15 a failed write left it
# there, so the next entry saved named a hash the store never got and the next open
# reported the store altered. Now the head returns to the failed entry's own
# predecessor — the last entry the store holds — and the next entry links to that.
# That is exact because no failure `patrastore_append` reports can follow a written row:
# libro refuses an over-long field before it touches patra, and every error patra's
# INSERT returns (`_exec_insert`, `tbl_insert`) comes before its page write
# (`lib/patra.cyr`, read at 1.15.1). A record refused before `chain_append` (a closed
# store) leaves the head alone. Either way the record is a gap, and counted.
#
# An audit failure does **not** fail the operation being audited. That is a
# deliberate choice and the opposite one is defensible: it means an action can
# succeed unrecorded. The counter is what keeps that visible rather than silent.

# libro's `det` column is a patra `COL_STR` — a fixed 256-byte slot holding 255
# bytes plus a NUL.
#
# ⚠ patra **refuses** an over-length value with `PATRA_ERR_ROWSZ` rather than
# truncating; its own audit (2026-08-18 S2-8) records that silent truncation
# "made two distinct values indistinguishable on disk". So an over-long detail
# would lose the whole entry, not the tail. This module truncates first, with a
# visible marker, so the record survives and says that it was cut.
var AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_DETAIL_MAX = 255;

# The `source` every Agnostic audit entry carries. libro spends patra's single
# index on this column, so it is what a query can filter by cheaply.
var AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_SRC = str_from("agnostic");
var AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_CUT = "…[cut]";

# The durable store, the streaming chain, and the counters.
var _agnostic_audit_store = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_chain = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_dropped = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_appended = 0;

# The integrity verdict taken when the store is opened.
#
# ## The off-thread fault this module was shaped around is FIXED
#
# `patrastore_open` used to prepare its `SELECT` and `COUNT` once and cache the
# handles in the store struct, while patra's SQL parse scratch is **per-thread**.
# A statement parsed on the opening thread and executed on a sandhi pool worker
# reached into TLS that was not there and **killed the process** — no diagnostic,
# no unwind. This tree found it the hard way ("the first HTTP request to one
# endpoint takes the whole server down while every other route keeps working"),
# filed it, and worked around it by reading nothing off the main thread.
#
# **Fixed in libro 2.8.9**, which reaches `lib/` transitively through agnosai
# 2.0.5: both read paths now parse on the **calling** thread, and libro carries
# `test_patrastore_read_from_another_thread` as a regression guard — mutation-
# verified upstream to SIGSEGV if the cache is restored.
#
# So the work-around is gone: `agnostic_audit_count` queries live, and
# `agnostic_audit_reverify` can run on any thread. Kept as history because the
# failure mode was a process kill with no diagnostic, and a future reader adding
# a cache here should know what it costs.
#
# ⚠ **The open-time verdict is still what `/api/v1/audit` reports by default**,
# and that remains deliberate. The question a tamper-evident chain answers is
# "was this file altered while I was not running", which is exactly what start-up
# sees. A live re-verify re-reads and re-hashes the whole chain, so it is a
# request-scoped cost that grows with the trail — offered as a function, not
# wired into a hot path.
var _agnostic_audit_intact = 1;
var _agnostic_audit_bad_index = 0 - 1;
var _agnostic_audit_at_open = 0;

# ## The verdict says what it found (0.1.15)
#
# Until 0.1.15 the open ran libro's `verify_chain`, which stops at the first failure, so a
# broken trail reported one index and nothing after it: a real alteration later in the file
# went unreported, and a trail with a harmless break could never say so. Two kinds of break
# are now told apart, and the whole trail is walked (`_agnostic_audit_judge_locked`):
#
#   * **restart** — an entry that hashes correctly, past the first, whose `prev_hash` is
#     empty: a chain that began again without linking to the trail before it. Every trail a
#     release before 0.1.13 wrote has one after each restart that recorded something
#     (0.1.13 seeds the head at open), and so does any start whose read of the trail failed
#     (below). ⚠ It is not proof of innocence: the entries just before such a break could
#     have been deleted and nothing would show it.
#   * **altered** — anything else: an entry whose hash does not match its content, a first
#     entry that names a predecessor (the front was cut off), or a link to anything but the
#     entry before it (deleted, inserted or reordered).
#
# A read that fails is a third outcome, **unverified**: until 0.1.15 libro's legacy load
# answered an empty vec for a failed query, which `verify_chain` passed, so an unreadable
# trail reported `intact: true` with nothing checked.
enum AgnosticAuditVerdict {
    AGNOSTIC_AV_INTACT     = 0;     # every entry hashes and links
    AGNOSTIC_AV_RESTARTS   = 1;     # breaks, and every one a restart
    AGNOSTIC_AV_ALTERED    = 2;     # at least one alteration
    AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED = 3;     # the trail could not be read
}

enum AgnosticAuditBreak {
    AGNOSTIC_AB_RESTART = 1;
    AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED = 2;
}

# The breaks kept for reading, first first, as (index, kind) pairs; how many are kept; how
# many there are in all; and how many of those are alterations.
var AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_BREAKS_KEPT = 32;
var _agnostic_audit_breaks: i64[64];
var _agnostic_audit_break_kept = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_break_total = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_altered = 0;
var _agnostic_audit_verdict = 0;

# ## The newest entries, kept for reading (0.1.10)
#
# `GET /api/v1/audit/entries` shows the trail's newest entries — the Audit view. libro reads
# only the WHOLE store (`patrastore_load_all`), into the global heap, which has no `free()`:
# serving a page that way would leak the size of the trail on every request. So the newest
# `AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING` entries are kept here, COPIED into fixed slots allocated once — seeded
# at open from the same load that verifies the chain, and filled as each entry is persisted,
# under the audit lock. Older entries stay in the store, and verification covers them; this
# route does not serve them.
#
# A slot holds an entry's position in the chain (`index`, from 0), its severity, and its text
# fields, each an 8-byte length and then at most its field's bytes. A field longer than its
# slot is cut at a UTF-8 boundary and the slot says so (`CUT`) — the stored entry, which is
# the evidence, is never touched.
var AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING = 1024;

enum AgnosticAuditSlotOff {
    AGNOSTIC_AS_INDEX   = 0;
    AGNOSTIC_AS_SEV     = 8;
    AGNOSTIC_AS_CUT     = 16;
    AGNOSTIC_AS_TS      = 24;    # 8 + 32
    AGNOSTIC_AS_SRC     = 64;    # 8 + 64
    AGNOSTIC_AS_ACTION  = 136;   # 8 + 64
    AGNOSTIC_AS_DETAILS = 208;   # 8 + 320: a detail is at most AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_DETAIL_MAX
    AGNOSTIC_AS_HASH    = 536;   # 8 + 64
    AGNOSTIC_AS_PREV    = 608;   # 8 + 64
    AGNOSTIC_AS_SIZE    = 680;
}

var _agnostic_audit_ring = 0;          # AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING slots, allocated once
var _agnostic_audit_ring_next = 0;     # the index the next persisted entry gets
var _agnostic_audit_ring_floor = 0;    # the lowest index ever put in the ring

# ## ⛔ One append at a time — the chain and its handle share one lock
#
# An append is three steps: `chain_append` reads the head hash and links the new
# entry to it, `patrastore_append` persists the entry, and the counters move.
# libro takes no lock in any of them, and until 0.1.4 neither did this module —
# with up to `AGNOSTIC_WORKERS` workers recording at once. Two appends that
# interleave the first step link to the SAME predecessor (a fork); two that
# interleave the second persist out of chain order. Either way the next
# verification reports tampering nobody did, and the counters lose increments.
# The store's patra handle is shared by those workers too, with the same
# concurrent-SELECT hazard `src/engine/store.cyr` describes.
#
# So every function below that touches the chain, the handle or the counters
# runs under `_agnostic_audit_rl`. It is separate from the store lock and never
# held at the same time as it — neither module calls the other while holding its
# own.
var _agnostic_audit_rl = agnostic_rlock_new();

# Release the audit lock; an unbalanced release is logged and releases nothing.
fn _agnostic_audit_unlock(): i64 {
    if (agnostic_rlock_exit(_agnostic_audit_rl) == 1) { return 1; }
    agnostic_log_kv_int(SK_ERROR, "audit lock released by a thread that does not hold it", 53,
      "tid", 3, gettid());
    return 0;
}

# Open the audit store and start a chain. Mount time only.
#
# Returns 1 on success, 0 when the store cannot be opened. The caller decides
# whether that is fatal; `agnostic_serve_mount` treats it as fatal, because a
# product tier that silently stops auditing is worse than one that refuses to
# start.
fn agnostic_audit_open(path: Str): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var r = _agnostic_audit_open_locked(path);
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return r;
}

# `agnostic_audit_open`'s body. The caller holds the audit lock.
fn _agnostic_audit_open_locked(path: Str): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_store != 0) { return 1; }
    var s = patrastore_open(path);
    if (s == 0) { return 0; }
    # One index per table is patra's limit and libro spends it on `src`, so a
    # query by source is indexed and anything else is a scan. Nothing here
    # queries by anything else.
    patrastore_create_source_index(s);
    _agnostic_audit_store = s;
    _agnostic_audit_chain = chain_new_streaming();
    _agnostic_audit_dropped = 0;
    _agnostic_audit_appended = 0;

    # The verification pass at open — the "was it altered while I was not
    # running" question. No longer the ONLY possible pass; see
    # `agnostic_audit_reverify`.
    _agnostic_audit_at_open = patrastore_len(s);
    # ⚠ `_or_err`, not `patrastore_load_all`: the legacy load answers an empty vec for a
    # query that failed, which reads as an empty, intact trail (0.1.15).
    var entries = patrastore_load_all_or_err(s);
    var verdict = _agnostic_audit_judge_locked(entries);
    if (verdict == AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED) { entries = 0; }
    _agnostic_audit_ring_seed_locked(entries);
    _agnostic_audit_seed_head_locked(entries);
    # ⚠ Loud, and deliberately not fatal. Refusing to start would hand an attacker a denial
    # of service by corrupting one byte, and would take the evidence offline with it. The
    # verdict is logged, surfaced on `/api/v1/audit`, and the process keeps recording so
    # whatever happened is bracketed rather than hidden.
    _agnostic_audit_log_verdict(0);
    return 1;
}

# Judge loaded trail `entries` — a vec in chain order, or libro's error object from
# `patrastore_load_all_or_err` — and record the verdict, the breaks and the first bad index.
# Answers the verdict. The caller holds the audit lock.
#
# Walks every entry, never stopping at a break (see "The verdict says what it found").
fn _agnostic_audit_judge_locked(entries): i64 {
    _agnostic_audit_break_kept = 0;
    _agnostic_audit_break_total = 0;
    _agnostic_audit_altered = 0;
    _agnostic_audit_bad_index = 0 - 1;
    if (entries == 0 || libro_is_error(entries) == 1) {
        _agnostic_audit_intact = 0;
        _agnostic_audit_verdict = AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED;
        return AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED;
    }
    var n = vec_len(entries);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var e = vec_get(entries, i);
        var kind = 0;
        var ph = entry_prev_hash(e);
        if (entry_verify(e) == 0) {
            kind = AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED;
        } elif (i == 0) {
            if (str_len(ph) != 0) { kind = AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED; }
        } elif (constant_time_eq_str(ph, entry_hash(vec_get(entries, i - 1))) == 0) {
            kind = AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED;
            if (str_len(ph) == 0) { kind = AGNOSTIC_AB_RESTART; }
        }
        if (kind != 0) { _agnostic_audit_note_break(i, kind); }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var v = AGNOSTIC_AV_INTACT;
    if (_agnostic_audit_break_total > 0) { v = AGNOSTIC_AV_RESTARTS; }
    if (_agnostic_audit_altered > 0) { v = AGNOSTIC_AV_ALTERED; }
    _agnostic_audit_verdict = v;
    _agnostic_audit_intact = 0;
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_INTACT) { _agnostic_audit_intact = 1; }
    return v;
}

# Count break `kind` at entry `index`, keeping it while there is room. The first break of
# any kind is `bad_index`.
fn _agnostic_audit_note_break(index, kind): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_break_total == 0) { _agnostic_audit_bad_index = index; }
    _agnostic_audit_break_total = _agnostic_audit_break_total + 1;
    if (kind == AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED) { _agnostic_audit_altered = _agnostic_audit_altered + 1; }
    if (_agnostic_audit_break_kept < AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_BREAKS_KEPT) {
        var slot = &_agnostic_audit_breaks + _agnostic_audit_break_kept * 16;
        store64(slot, index);
        store64(slot + 8, kind);
        _agnostic_audit_break_kept = _agnostic_audit_break_kept + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Log the verdict just taken: nothing when the trail is intact. `again` is 1 for a re-verify.
fn _agnostic_audit_log_verdict(again): i64 {
    var v = _agnostic_audit_verdict;
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_ALTERED) {
        if (again == 1) {
            agnostic_log_kv_int(SK_ERROR,
              "audit chain failed re-verification; the store was altered", 57,
              "entry", 5, _agnostic_audit_first_altered());
        } else {
            agnostic_log_kv_int(SK_ERROR,
              "audit chain failed verification at open; the store was altered", 62,
              "entry", 5, _agnostic_audit_first_altered());
        }
        return 1;
    }
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_RESTARTS) {
        agnostic_log_kv_int(SK_WARN,
          "audit trail has restart breaks (a release before 0.1.13, or a failed read); no alteration found", 95,
          "breaks", 6, _agnostic_audit_break_total);
        return 1;
    }
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED) {
        sakshi_error("audit trail could not be read; it is unverified and its head is unknown", 71);
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# The index of the first altered entry, or -1 when none is kept. Past the kept breaks an
# alteration is counted but not placed.
fn _agnostic_audit_first_altered(): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < _agnostic_audit_break_kept) {
        var slot = &_agnostic_audit_breaks + i * 16;
        if (load64(slot + 8) == AGNOSTIC_AB_ALTERED) { return load64(slot); }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

# The verdict taken at open, or by the last re-verify: an `AGNOSTIC_AV_*` (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_verdict(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_verdict;
}

# The verdict's wire name: `intact`, `restarts`, `altered` or `unverified` (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_verdict_name(v): i64 {
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_RESTARTS) { return "restarts"; }
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_ALTERED) { return "altered"; }
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_UNVERIFIED) { return "unverified"; }
    return "intact";
}

# How many breaks the last verdict found, kept or not (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_break_count(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_break_total;
}

# How many breaks are kept for reading: at most `AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_BREAKS_KEPT` (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_breaks_kept(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_break_kept;
}

# Kept break `i`'s entry index, or -1 past the last one kept (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_break_index(i): i64 {
    if (i < 0 || i >= _agnostic_audit_break_kept) { return 0 - 1; }
    return load64(&_agnostic_audit_breaks + i * 16);
}

# Kept break `i`'s kind — an `AGNOSTIC_AB_*` — or 0 past the last one kept (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_break_kind(i): i64 {
    if (i < 0 || i >= _agnostic_audit_break_kept) { return 0; }
    return load64(&_agnostic_audit_breaks + i * 16 + 8);
}

# A break kind's wire name: `restart` or `altered` (0.1.15).
fn agnostic_audit_break_kind_name(k): i64 {
    if (k == AGNOSTIC_AB_RESTART) { return "restart"; }
    return "altered";
}

# Link the chain's head to the last entry of `entries` (the store, in chain order), so the
# first entry this process appends names it as its predecessor. The caller holds the audit lock.
#
# ⚠ **Until 0.1.13 nothing did, and every restart broke the chain.** A streaming chain keeps
# its head in libro's prev-hash carry slot, which `chain_new_streaming` leaves empty, so the
# first append after an open linked to "" — the genesis link — and the NEXT open's
# `verify_chain` failed there: `intact: false`, from a file nobody touched.
#
# The value is the loaded entry's own hash Str. `patrastore_load_all` copies every field out
# of patra's result set into the global heap (`_ps_copy_cstr`), which never frees, so it
# outlives the load; the first append replaces it with that entry's hash.
#
# Seeded on a broken chain too: new entries continue the file as it is, and the verdict
# already names where it broke. A read that failed (`unverified`, 0.1.15) leaves the head
# empty, so the first entry this process writes starts a new chain, and the next open reports
# a restart break there.
fn _agnostic_audit_seed_head_locked(entries): i64 {
    if (entries == 0 || libro_is_error(entries) == 1) { return 0; }
    var n = vec_len(entries);
    if (n == 0) { return 0; }
    chain_set_prev_hash(_agnostic_audit_chain, entry_hash(vec_get(entries, n - 1)));
    return 1;
}

# Close the store, flushing anything batched.
#
# Takes the audit lock, so a close waits for the append in flight and every later
# one sees a closed trail rather than a handle torn down under it.
fn agnostic_audit_close(): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    if (_agnostic_audit_store != 0) {
        patrastore_close(_agnostic_audit_store);
        _agnostic_audit_store = 0;
        _agnostic_audit_chain = 0;
    }
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return 0;
}

# Whether the trail is recording.
fn agnostic_audit_is_open(): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_store == 0) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

# How many entries the store held when it was opened.
#
# Distinct from `agnostic_audit_count`, which is live — this one is the baseline
# the process started from, and is what tells an operator how much of the trail
# predates this run.
fn agnostic_audit_at_open(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_at_open;
}

# How many entries this process has appended.
fn agnostic_audit_appended(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_appended;
}

# How many entries the store holds, queried live.
#
# Was `at_open + appended` while libro's cached statements made a live read fault
# off the main thread. That is fixed (see `_agnostic_audit_intact`), so this asks
# the store — which is also correct when this process is **not** the only writer,
# where the counter would silently under-report.
#
# Falls back to the counters when the store is not open, so a caller still gets
# this process's own arithmetic rather than 0.
fn agnostic_audit_count(): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var n = 0;
    if (_agnostic_audit_store == 0) {
        n = _agnostic_audit_at_open + _agnostic_audit_appended;
    } else {
        n = patrastore_len(_agnostic_audit_store);
    }
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return n;
}

# Re-verify the persisted chain now, updating the stored verdict. Returns 1 when
# intact, 0 when not, and -1 when there is no store open.
#
# ⚠ **Safe on any thread** — that is the whole point of libro 2.8.9, and it is
# what makes a re-verify endpoint possible at all. Before it, calling this from a
# pool worker killed the process.
#
# ⚠ **Re-reads and re-hashes the ENTIRE chain.** Cost grows with the trail, so
# this is a deliberate operator action, not something to put on a health check.
#
# Holds the audit lock for the whole pass, so appends wait for it — the chain it
# verifies is a consistent snapshot, not one with a half-persisted tail.
fn agnostic_audit_reverify(): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var r = _agnostic_audit_reverify_locked();
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return r;
}

# `agnostic_audit_reverify`'s body. The caller holds the audit lock.
fn _agnostic_audit_reverify_locked(): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_store == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var v = _agnostic_audit_judge_locked(patrastore_load_all_or_err(_agnostic_audit_store));
    _agnostic_audit_log_verdict(1);
    if (v == AGNOSTIC_AV_INTACT) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# How many appends failed — gaps that verification cannot reveal.
fn agnostic_audit_dropped(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_dropped;
}

# Fit a detail string inside the column, marking it when it does not fit.
#
# Returns `details` unchanged when it fits. Otherwise a prefix plus a visible
# marker: the record survives, and it says on its face that it was cut. Silence
# was the alternative and it is the one this port refuses.
fn agnostic_audit_fit_detail(details: Str): i64 {
    if (details == 0) { return str_from(""); }
    if (str_len(details) <= AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_DETAIL_MAX) { return details; }
    var marker = str_from(AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_CUT);
    var keep = AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_DETAIL_MAX - str_len(marker);
    if (keep < 0) { keep = 0; }
    var sb = str_builder_new();
    str_builder_add(sb, str_sub(details, 0, keep));
    str_builder_add(sb, marker);
    return str_builder_build(sb);
}

# Record one event. Returns 1 when it was persisted, 0 when it was not.
#
# ⚠ **The return is checked by nobody on purpose, and the counter is why.** An
# audit failure must not fail the operation being audited, but it must not vanish
# either — so this logs at ERROR and increments `agnostic_audit_dropped`, which
# `/ready` and the verify route both surface.
fn agnostic_audit_record(severity, source: Str, action: Str, details: Str): i64 {
    var det = agnostic_audit_fit_detail(details);
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var r = _agnostic_audit_record_locked(severity, source, action, det);
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return r;
}

# `agnostic_audit_record`'s body. The caller holds the audit lock, which makes
# link, persist and count one step: no other append can take the head between
# this entry's link and its write.
fn _agnostic_audit_record_locked(severity, source: Str, action: Str, det: Str): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_store == 0) { return 0; }

    var e = chain_append(_agnostic_audit_chain, severity, source, action, det);
    if (e == 0) {
        _agnostic_audit_dropped = _agnostic_audit_dropped + 1;
        return 0;
    }

    var rc = patrastore_append(_agnostic_audit_store, e);
    if (rc == PATRA_OK) {
        _agnostic_audit_appended = _agnostic_audit_appended + 1;
        _agnostic_audit_ring_put_locked(e);
    }
    if (rc != PATRA_OK) {
        # ⚠ Put the head back on the last stored entry (0.1.15). `chain_append` moved it to
        # `e`, which the store never got; left there, the next entry would name a hash the
        # store does not hold, and the next open would report the trail altered. See the
        # module header for why no failed write can have landed.
        chain_set_prev_hash(_agnostic_audit_chain, entry_prev_hash(e));
        _agnostic_audit_dropped = _agnostic_audit_dropped + 1;
        agnostic_log_kv_str(SK_ERROR, "audit append failed; the trail has a gap", 40,
          "action", 6, action);
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

# 1 when the last verdict found every entry hashing and linking; 0 for any break, and for a
# trail that could not be read.
#
# ⚠ **Deliberately NOT `patrastore_load_and_verify`.** That function returns the
# entries vec on success and an error object on failure — **both non-zero
# pointers**, structurally indistinguishable, so the obvious `if (r != 0)` reads
# an integrity violation as a successful load. An integrity check whose failure
# can be mistaken for success is worse than none. Since 0.1.15 the trail is read with
# `patrastore_load_all_or_err`, checked with `libro_is_error`, and walked here.
fn agnostic_audit_is_intact(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_intact;
}

# The index of the first break of either kind, or -1 when there is none (an intact or an
# unverified trail).
#
# ⚠ Until 0.1.13 this read offset 24 of libro's error, which libro 2.8.11 made `field_name`,
# so every broken chain was reported as broken at entry 0. Since 0.1.15 the index is this
# module's own, from its walk.
fn agnostic_audit_bad_index(): i64 {
    return _agnostic_audit_bad_index;
}

# Build a `key=value` detail, bounded by construction.
#
# Details are short and structured on purpose: the column holds 255 bytes, and a
# record that has to be cut is a record that lost something. Callers compose
# these rather than formatting prose.
#
# From the global heap, which never frees: for a caller with no request arena — the
# start-up sweep, a crew thread. A request path uses `agnostic_audit_detail_a`.
fn agnostic_audit_detail(key, value: Str): i64 {
    return agnostic_audit_detail_a(default_alloc(), key, value);
}

# `agnostic_audit_detail` in `a` — a request's arena (0.1.15). Safe because nothing keeps
# the detail past the record: libro binds it into the store write and the streaming chain
# keeps only the entry's hash, and the kept ring copies it into its own slots.
fn agnostic_audit_detail_a(a, key, value: Str): i64 {
    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    if (sb == 0) { return 0; }
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, key);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "=");
    if (value != 0) { str_builder_add_a(a, sb, value); }
    return str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
}

# --- the newest entries (0.1.10) -------------------------------------------

# How many of `n` bytes at `d` fit in `cap` without splitting a UTF-8 sequence.
fn _agnostic_audit_fit_utf8(d, n, cap): i64 {
    if (n <= cap) { return n; }
    var k = cap;
    # Back off past continuation bytes (10xxxxxx) to the start of the cut character.
    while (k > 0 && (load8(d + k) & 192) == 128) { k = k - 1; }
    return k;
}

# Copy Str `v` into the slot field at `f` (an 8-byte length, then `cap` bytes). Returns 1
# when it had to be cut.
fn _agnostic_audit_slot_text(f, cap, v: Str): i64 {
    if (v == 0) { store64(f, 0); return 0; }
    var n = str_len(v);
    var k = _agnostic_audit_fit_utf8(str_data(v), n, cap);
    memcpy(f + 8, str_data(v), k);
    store64(f, k);
    if (k < n) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# Put a persisted entry into the ring as the next index. The caller holds the audit lock.
fn _agnostic_audit_ring_put_locked(e): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_ring == 0 || e == 0) { return 0; }
    var idx = _agnostic_audit_ring_next;
    var slot = _agnostic_audit_ring + (idx % AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING) * AGNOSTIC_AS_SIZE;
    store64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_INDEX, idx);
    store64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_SEV, entry_severity(e));
    var cut = 0;
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_TS, 32, entry_timestamp(e));
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_SRC, 64, entry_source(e));
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_ACTION, 64, entry_action(e));
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_DETAILS, 320, entry_details(e));
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_HASH, 64, entry_hash(e));
    cut = cut | _agnostic_audit_slot_text(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_PREV, 64, entry_prev_hash(e));
    store64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_CUT, cut);
    _agnostic_audit_ring_next = idx + 1;
    return 1;
}

# Seed the ring at open with the newest entries of `entries` (the store, in chain order). The
# caller holds the audit lock. The ring is allocated on the first open and reused after.
fn _agnostic_audit_ring_seed_locked(entries): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_ring == 0) {
        _agnostic_audit_ring = alloc(AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING * AGNOSTIC_AS_SIZE);
        if (_agnostic_audit_ring == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    var n = 0;
    if (entries != 0) { n = vec_len(entries); }
    # A load that came back short of the count (a failed read reads as empty) holds
    # nothing: the next index is still the store's count, so indexes stay positions.
    if (n != _agnostic_audit_at_open) {
        _agnostic_audit_ring_next = _agnostic_audit_at_open;
        _agnostic_audit_ring_floor = _agnostic_audit_at_open;
        return 0;
    }
    var start = 0;
    if (n > AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING) { start = n - AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING; }
    _agnostic_audit_ring_next = start;
    _agnostic_audit_ring_floor = start;
    var i = start;
    while (i < n) {
        _agnostic_audit_ring_put_locked(vec_get(entries, i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 1;
}

# The lowest index `agnostic_audit_entries_a` can answer: entries below it are in the store,
# verified, and not kept for reading.
fn agnostic_audit_oldest_held(): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var lo = _agnostic_audit_ring_floor;
    if (_agnostic_audit_ring_next - AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING > lo) { lo = _agnostic_audit_ring_next - AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING; }
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return lo;
}

# How many entries the chain holds as this process has seen it: the next index.
fn agnostic_audit_next_index(): i64 {
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var n = _agnostic_audit_ring_next;
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return n;
}

# One entry read out of the ring, in an arena — 80 bytes.
enum AgnosticAuditReadOff {
    AGNOSTIC_AR_INDEX   = 0;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_SEV     = 8;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_CUT     = 16;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_TS      = 24;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_SRC     = 32;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_ACTION  = 40;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_DETAILS = 48;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_HASH    = 56;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_PREV    = 64;
    AGNOSTIC_AR_SIZE    = 72;
}

# A read entry's field.
fn agnostic_audit_read_get(r, off): i64 { return load64(r + off); }

# A slot's text field at `f` as a Str in `a`, or 0 on exhaustion.
fn _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, f): i64 {
    var n = load64(f);
    var buf = alloc_via(a, n + 1);
    if (buf == 0) { return 0; }
    memcpy(buf, f + 8, n);
    store8(buf + n, 0);
    return str_new_a(a, buf, n);
}

# Up to `limit` entries with an index below `before` (any, when `before` is past the end),
# newest first, as a vec of read entries copied into `a` — the slots may be overwritten as
# soon as the lock is released, so nothing here points into them. 0 on exhaustion.
fn agnostic_audit_entries_a(a, before, limit): i64 {
    var out = vec_new_a(a);
    if (out == 0) { return 0; }
    agnostic_rlock_enter(_agnostic_audit_rl);
    var r = _agnostic_audit_entries_locked_a(a, before, limit, out);
    _agnostic_audit_unlock();
    return r;
}

# `agnostic_audit_entries_a`'s body. The caller holds the audit lock.
fn _agnostic_audit_entries_locked_a(a, before, limit, out): i64 {
    if (_agnostic_audit_ring == 0) { return out; }
    var hi = before;
    if (hi > _agnostic_audit_ring_next) { hi = _agnostic_audit_ring_next; }
    var lo = _agnostic_audit_ring_floor;
    if (_agnostic_audit_ring_next - AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING > lo) { lo = _agnostic_audit_ring_next - AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING; }
    var idx = hi - 1;
    while (idx >= lo && vec_len(out) < limit) {
        var slot = _agnostic_audit_ring + (idx % AGNOSTIC_AUDIT_RING) * AGNOSTIC_AS_SIZE;
        var r = alloc_via(a, AGNOSTIC_AR_SIZE);
        if (r == 0) { return 0; }
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_INDEX, load64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_INDEX));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_SEV, load64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_SEV));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_CUT, load64(slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_CUT));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_TS, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_TS));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_SRC, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_SRC));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_ACTION, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_ACTION));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_DETAILS, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_DETAILS));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_HASH, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_HASH));
        store64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_PREV, _agnostic_audit_slot_str_a(a, slot + AGNOSTIC_AS_PREV));
        if (load64(r + AGNOSTIC_AR_PREV) == 0) { return 0; }
        vec_push_a(a, out, r);
        idx = idx - 1;
    }
    return out;
}

# A severity's wire name — libro's, lowercased.
fn agnostic_audit_severity_wire(sev): i64 {
    if (sev == SEV_DEBUG) { return "debug"; }
    if (sev == SEV_INFO) { return "info"; }
    if (sev == SEV_WARNING) { return "warning"; }
    if (sev == SEV_ERROR) { return "error"; }
    if (sev == SEV_CRITICAL) { return "critical"; }
    if (sev == SEV_SECURITY) { return "security"; }
    return "unknown";
}
